WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.200
Abraham: As we all know the internet
is available almost everywhere and

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.540
can connect people all over the world,
but this makes it very hard to police.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.093
Nicole: Threats can come from anywhere
and tracking the threat actors down

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.749
can be very difficult especially when
they are acting in another country.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:20.020
Abraham: It is for this reason that
cyberspace would benefit from being

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.570
regulated by international law but how
this could be done is a real challenge

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.520
especially as many nation states
have differing views on the subject.

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:32.520
Nicole: There are currently no clear
answers on how this could be facilitated

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.480
but there are some clear trends.

00:00:34.779 --> 00:00:39.519
Some states are in favour of creating
international institutions like the United

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.890
Nations, set up to regulate cyberspace,
other think it should remain unregulated.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.769
Many nation states feel that they should
be able to apply their own regulations.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:55.970
Abraham: In 2015 all the 193 members
of the United Nations, agreed

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.000
that international Law should
apply in cyberspace, but how these

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.180
laws should be implemented and
negotiated is still up for debate.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.830
Nicole: Different nations interpret
how to apply international laws in

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.379
different ways as they interpret
other laws in different ways.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.020
Abraham: It has become evident over the
last few years that many nation states see

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.380
the use of cyberspace differently and some
offer more freedom to its use than others.

00:01:22.099 --> 00:01:26.039
Some see ‘digital mass surveillance’
as a violation of human rights,

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:30.040
but others see it as a way of
monitoring the population and helping

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.590
to maintain a peaceful society!

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:36.160
Nicole: What happens if one state
‘hacks” another state, does that

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.880
mean that they have the right
of self-defence to hack back?

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.619
Who is going to police this?

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.550
There are some organisations other
than the UN out there trying to

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.379
resolve these problems such as:

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.149
Abraham: The Paris Call for Trust and
Security in Cyberspace who invited

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.990
states and the private sector, the
world of research and civil society

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.819
to work together to adopt fundamental
principles and responsible behaviour.

00:02:00.359 --> 00:02:03.530
Nicole: The Cybersecurity Tech
Accord which works to foster

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.800
collaboration amongst global companies
to defend themselves from threat.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.929
Abraham: The Global Commission on
the Stability of Cyberspace which

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.870
includes international governments,
businesses and organisations helps to

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.630
promote awareness and understanding
on issues of cyber security.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.340
Nicole: Many similarly minded countries
show a willingness to get together

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.140
to address the goal of creating
international laws and regulations

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.220
in cyberspace but unless some of the
other major players get on board this

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.150
has little chance of becoming law.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.120
However, some individual countries
feel that they should have the right

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.469
to control their own cyberspace free
from international interference.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.109
Abraham: Some of these countries
are large players in cyberspace such

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.140
as Russia, China and Saudi Arabia.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.780
This can be seen quite clearly
with the current global unrest

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.220
issues which are changing the
face of cyberspace in the world.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.059
Nicole: Some countries also want
to have more control over digital

00:03:00.059 --> 00:03:04.650
monopolies and be able to regulate and
tax some of the larger tech companies.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.120
Some governments also want tech
companies and internet service providers

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:14.509
to restrict and monitor some content
whilst still offering freedom of speech.

00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:17.689
Abraham: Net neutrality is
also a cause for concern.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.790
It means that internet service
providers must treat all content

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.690
flowing through their systems the same.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.090
Companies shouldn’t be able to block
access to any services and should

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.540
keep the internet as an open field
which is crucial for innovation.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.380
Nicole: Zero rating on the other hand is
the practice of providing free internet

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.280
services under certain conditions.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.829
There are no current international laws
covering ‘Net neutrality’ and ‘zero

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:47.620
rating’ but many feel these fall into
the category of ‘human rights laws’ which

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.609
apply to many countries around the world.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.489
Abraham: We seem a long way off from
having an international cyberspace

00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:57.700
law as it stands today and if every
country or nation state has its own

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:01.209
laws then it would be practically
impossible to prosecute anyone

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.760
for international cyber crimes.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.510
Nicole: So, as you can see the
whole debate about international

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.700
laws and cyberspace is rather
a tangled web, pardon the pun.

